<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Utendi wa Tambuka</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1728">1728</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Chuo cha Tambuka</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Utendi wa Tambuka is an epic poem in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> composed in 1728 at the court of Pate, on the coast of present-day Kenya. It tells of a war between the forces of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prophet-muhammad">Prophet Muhammad</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> at Tabuk, and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> is the oldest dated work of Swahili literature. Written in Arabic script, it uses the utendi metre that became standard in Swahili narrative poetry.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/utendi-wa-tambuka.jpg" alt="Utendi wa Tambuka" width="320" height="200" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 19th century manuscript, Mwana, Kupona, 1810-1860 ( Author, Primary ), Kijuma, Muhammad ( scribe ) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Utenzi_wa_Hirqal_(MS_45022a).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
